In article <9004292211.AA02179@cie.uoregon.edu> ghudson@CIE.UOREGON.EDU writes:
>:Engraving...
>     Looking through the executable, I noted that there was a string for
>"There is something written here in a very strange way."  I wonder what the
>stats are for that method of writing (obviously, it's an error report and
>you should never see it, but it'd be interesting)....

This is an "impossible" message not obtainable by any normal means. If you
see it, there is either a new bug somewhere else in the code, a 
system-dependent code problem, or possibly a hardware problem. You will be
advised to quit at that point (although you don't have to). There are many
such "sanity checks" in the code. Their purpose is largely diagnostic, but
occasionally, if players see this message, save their game, and restart it,
the problem goes away. In such a case, please report it anyway.

>     Oh yes.  A pet peeve-- if you're blinded and have telepathy, "it hits"
>and "you hit it" are inappropriate, since you can identify monsters with the
>/ command.

I think this peeve is reversed. It's the '/' command that is questionable in 
this case, not the messages.

>     When a rust monster or dwarf eats away at the edges of a room, the
>square turns blank instead of to a doorway symbol.  When it eats away at a
>passage next to you (edge of room or just rock), the square also turns blank,
>and you must either move onto the square or move away from the square and back
>onto another square next to it before it registers as passageway.  Notably,
>whenever one moves onto the border of a room, all of the changed squares
>register as being doorways.

(Rust monsters eating rock?)

This is a bug that was introduced into PL7 while trying to fix something else.
It receives a band-aid in PL8. Watch for better overall treatment of display
in 3.1.

>     And shouldn't peaceful dwarves refrain from doing a furniture 
>rearrangement on the level?  

Why? The player doesn't own the level; in fact, he or she is not even native to
it, and it is in the nature of dwarves etc. to dig for minerals, gems etc. What
harm does it do you for the level to be dug out (other than the display turning
ugly)?
